{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791834403,"nonce":"/OKhkVKLv80FahkAwsJXGEl77nM=","message":"Infrastructure checkpoint completed in 3914b6eb3e307b3088ffc52cc2f19e37c742ad92.\n\nDesign: ash_core::generated::prepare builds a fresh DecodedBytecode snapshot before runtime tables, AIR caches or tier threads exist. All IDs are reserved deterministically in key order before any body builder runs, allowing recursive references. Keys include pass/version, source IDs and specialization choices; identical declarations deduplicate, conflicting signatures refuse. AIR is verified, direct-call signatures and register mappings are checked, private constants are rebased once, source positions are retained in per-op debug tables, and functions are appended through normal body-fact accounting. Failure leaves input untouched. Growth is bounded by helper count, body/total ops and owned AIR size.\n\nHelpers become ordinary functions shared by interpreter, Cranelift and LLVM, with no per-tier synthesis. Prepared module generations enter the canonical AIR cache key, so allocator reuse of the same module address cannot return stale helpers. Deduplication is snapshot-local; changed code/types/host metadata require rebuilding from original input. Host registration on prepared modules and hot reload are explicitly refused until dependency-aware invalidation exists. Existing OSR uses the ordinary interpreter-configured canonical AIR/PC/register mapping.\n\nDevelopment-only --dev-generated-helper INDEX clones a scalar function, redirects its self calls and puts a forwarding wrapper at its original ID. The fixture's generated function 385 reaches both Cranelift and LLVM in automatic hybrid mode and produces checksum 1313310000, matching interpreter and single-tier executions. AOT decodes separately for the target ABI: direct probe plus --build/--emit-aot is explicitly refused. --emit-optimized writes the prepared ordinary functions; native AOT runs that artifact correctly and wasm32-wasip1 object validation succeeds. Wasm execution was not tested at this checkpoint. The existing encoder drops debug information; IDs/signatures/bodies survive, preparation metadata is process-local.\n\nValidation:\n- cargo test --offline -p ash_core --lib generated::tests: 7 passed.\n- cargo test --offline -p ash_core --lib reload::tests: 9 passed.\n- cargo test --offline -p ash --test generated_helpers: 5 passed (interpreter, Cranelift, LLVM, automatic hybrid installs, emitted bytecode, native AOT, wasm object validation, explicit mode refusal).\n- cargo test --offline -p ash --no-default-features --test generated_helpers: 4 passed. This was before the final register-validation guard; that guard is covered by the final core/default-feature reruns.\n- rustfmt --check on new Rust files and git diff --check: passed.\n\nInitial scope is portable i32 scalar helpers; references, native calls, exception regions, pinned cells, SIMD scratch and inlined debug frames are refused. This is registration infrastructure, not allocation-elision authorization. Future fusion must establish runtime allocation policy and must not export runtime-dependent assumptions through metadata-free .hl serialization.\n\nBenchBinaryTrees.hx and bench_binary_trees.hl remain byte-identical to baseline (source d705a3b8c01dd2dcae7a89e27cf814317df8ba50ccbf3ec6d1ea8bfdcfd669b5; bytecode 9698cecaf8852fa219acfd3361ea8659543cb7dc5a4033168aa3ff96d01a926d). No allocation reduction or speedup is claimed.\n\nNext: bfd0a53, fusion transformation. Parent b002f3f remains open.","files":null}]}